1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,270
Governments run short of
money all the time.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:07,230
They have bills to pay, workers to
pay, and debts to pay back.

3
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:10,450
And every government has
something no one else has.

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:12,140
A printing press.

5
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:14,010
So here's the obvious fix.

6
00:00:14,260 --> 00:00:15,370
Print more money.

7
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:19,270
Hand everyone a stack of cash,
and poverty is solved.

8
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:21,210
It sounds too good to be true.

9
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:22,230
And it is.

10
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,130
But the reason is surprisingly simple, and
it has nothing to do with paper

11
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:28,010
or ink.

12
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:30,890
Let's start with what money actually is.

13
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:32,890
Money isn't wealth.

14
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:36,730
You can't eat a banknote, live in
it, or drive it to work.

15
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:38,530
Money is a ticket.

16
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:41,900
It's a ticket you trade for real
things, like bread,

17
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:44,790
shoes, fuel and somebody's time.

18
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,310
The real wealth is the stuff.

19
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:51,270
And printing more tickets doesn't
bake a single extra loaf.

20
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:53,030
Picture a tiny town.

21
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:57,650
It has ten loaves of bread, and all the people
together have a hundred dollars.

22
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:02,080
If everyone spends their money on bread,
each loaf ends up costing about

23
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:03,340
ten dollars.

24
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,550
Now the town prints another hundred
dollars and hands it out.

25
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:08,350
Everyone feels richer.

26
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,960
But there are still only ten loaves.

27
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:12,560
So what happens?

28
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:15,200
People line up with more
money than before,

29
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:16,650
and the baker notices.

30
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,650
The price climbs toward twenty
dollars a loaf.

31
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:23,770
After all that printing, nobody can
buy more bread than before.

32
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:25,450
That's inflation.

33
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:28,270
More money chasing the same
amount of stuff.

34
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:31,300
Printing isn't the only thing
that pushes prices up.

35
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:34,940
A bad harvest or more expensive
oil can do it too,

36
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,540
because then there's less
stuff to go around.

37
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:41,580
But the amount of money is something a
government and its central bank control

38
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:42,470
directly.

39
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:44,430
And printing isn't even fair.

40
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:48,670
Whoever gets the new money first can
spend it before prices rise.

41
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,890
Wages often catch up later.

42
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:55,040
And anyone with savings sitting in the
bank pays the higher prices with

43
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:57,240
money that's now worth less.

44
00:01:57,320 --> 00:01:59,550
Now push that idea much further.

45
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:04,460
Economists call it hyperinflation when
prices rise more than fifty percent

46
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:05,640
in a single month.

47
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:09,000
And history has run this
experiment more than once.

48
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:11,320
Germany, 1923.

49
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:15,570
After the First World War, Germany owed
huge reparation payments.

50
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:20,640
In early 1923, French and Belgian
troops occupied the Ruhr,

51
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:24,360
Germany's industrial heart, because
payments had fallen behind.

52
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:27,450
German workers there stopped
working in protest.

53
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:31,400
And the government paid for that passive
resistance by printing money.

54
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:36,160
At the end of 1922, a loaf of bread in
Berlin cost around a hundred and

55
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:37,170
sixty marks.

56
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:42,440
By late 1923, it cost around two
hundred billion marks.

57
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:45,760
People's savings, built up
over a lifetime,

58
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:47,090
became worthless.

59
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:51,780
The crisis ended in November 1923
with a new currency,

60
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:52,910
the Rentenmark.

61
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:57,220
One new Rentenmark was worth one
trillion old marks.

62
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,680
You might think the world
learned its lesson.

63
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:00,740
It didn't.

64
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:03,000
Zimbabwe, 2008.

65
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:07,000
From the year 2000, the government
seized large commercial farms,

66
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:09,160
and farm output collapsed.

67
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:13,000
The country was producing far less, and the
government kept printing money

68
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:14,380
to pay its bills.

69
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:15,580
Fewer goods.

70
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:16,540
More money.

71
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:18,440
You already know where that goes.

72
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:23,560
By 2007, prices were rising so fast that the
government tried the next obvious

73
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:24,440
fix.

74
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:29,930
In June 2007, it ordered shops to cut
their prices roughly in half.

75
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:34,227
Police arrested thousands of shop owners
and managers who didn't comply.

76
00:03:34,247 --> 00:03:39,600
Within days, bread, milk, meat and cornmeal
disappeared from the shelves.

77
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:42,613
Selling at those prices meant
selling at a loss,

78
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:45,347
so shops ran out and weren't restocked.

79
00:03:45,367 --> 00:03:48,800
A low price on an empty shelf
doesn't feed anyone.

80
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:50,533
So the printing went on.

81
00:03:50,553 --> 00:03:55,680
In November 2008, economists estimate prices
in Zimbabwe were doubling about

82
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:58,227
every twenty-five hours.

83
00:03:58,247 --> 00:04:01,700
Imagine the price of your
groceries doubling every day.

84
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:07,647
In January 2009, the central bank issued a
one hundred trillion dollar note.

85
00:04:07,667 --> 00:04:11,860
A few months later, Zimbabwe gave up on its
own currency and started using

86
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:14,640
US dollars and other foreign
money instead.

87
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:16,560
And it still isn't the record.

88
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:21,387
That belongs to Hungary in 1946, right
after the Second World War.

89
00:04:21,407 --> 00:04:26,080
At the peak, prices there doubled
about every fifteen hours.

90
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:29,340
Look at these stories together and
you see the same pattern.

91
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:32,353
The amount of real stuff
shrank or stalled,

92
00:04:32,373 --> 00:04:34,820
and the amount of money exploded.

93
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:37,460
So should we never print money at all?

94
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:38,500
Not quite.

95
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:40,520
Printing money does have a job.

96
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:44,520
When an economy grows, people make more
things and do more work.

97
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:47,000
If the amount of money never grew with it,

98
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:48,807
prices would slowly fall.

99
00:04:48,827 --> 00:04:53,140
That sounds nice, but falling prices can
make people delay spending and

100
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:54,887
make debts harder to pay.

101
00:04:54,907 --> 00:04:57,753
That's why central banks add
money carefully.

102
00:04:57,773 --> 00:05:02,020
The US Federal Reserve, for example, aims for
inflation of about two percent

103
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:03,760
a year over the long run.

104
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:07,067
Prices rise a little, slowly
and predictably.

105
00:05:07,087 --> 00:05:11,630
The trouble starts when money grows much
faster than the things it can buy.

106
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:13,820
So why don't we just print more money?

107
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:16,720
Because money is a ticket,
not the treasure.

108
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:20,000
Print more tickets, and each
ticket buys less.

109
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:24,300
Push it too far, and a loaf of bread can
cost two hundred billion.

110
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,647
More money doesn't make more bread.

111
00:05:26,667 --> 00:05:28,790
It just makes bread cost more.

